# Getting Started

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    if File.regular?(Path.expand("../mix.exs", __DIR__)) do
      {:egglog_elixir, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
    else
      {:egglog_elixir, git: "https://github.com/davfuenmayor/egglog_elixir.git", branch: "main"}
    end,
    {:kino, "~> 0.19.0"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)

# Builds snapshot views with native output and automatic graph rendering.
defmodule EgglogLivebook.View do
  def snapshot(snapshot) do
    output = Enum.map(Egglog.Output.texts(snapshot.result), &Kino.Text.new/1)

    graph =
      case snapshot do
        %{format: :mermaid, mermaid: source} -> Kino.Mermaid.new(source)
        %{format: :svg, svg: svg} -> Kino.HTML.new(svg)
        %{text: text} -> Kino.Text.new(text)
      end

    notes =
      if snapshot.omitted in [nil, ""],
        do: [],
        else: [Kino.Markdown.new("Partial view: " <> snapshot.omitted)]

    empty =
      if snapshot.stats.snapshot_nodes == 0,
        do: [Kino.Markdown.new("The e-graph is empty.")],
        else: []

    Kino.Layout.grid(output ++ notes ++ empty ++ [graph])
  end
end

snapshot_options = [
  render: :auto,
  snapshot_max_functions: 12,
  snapshot_max_calls_per_function: 6,
  snapshot_inline_leaves: 1
]

view = fn title, snapshot ->
  Kino.Layout.grid([
    Kino.Markdown.new("**" <> title <> "**"),
    EgglogLivebook.View.snapshot(snapshot)
  ])
end

Kino.nothing()
```

## Using EgglogElixir

[back to index](index.livemd)

Note on setup cell: loads EgglogElixir and Kino and defines automatic snapshot displays.

This guide shows how to use the library in an application: choose an execution
model, obtain structured results, handle errors, and close native resources.
The same arithmetic example makes the two models directly comparable.

In a Mix application, add the wrapper to `mix.exs` with a pinned version or reviewed
Git revision. `Mix.install` and the graph display helpers above are for this
notebook; application code needs neither Livebook nor Kino.

## Two execution models

| Model            | How it works                                                                        |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Egglog.EGraph`  | Calls mutate the same native e-graph. `push` saves a checkpoint; `pop` restores it. |
| `Egglog.Program` | Load a base once. Every run uses a fresh native clone, leaving the base unchanged.  |

Use EGraph for incremental exploration, Program for independent experiments.
Both delegate reasoning to native egglog.

## One example: simplify `(2 × 3) + 0`

Both models use the same declarations, rules, input and schedule. Constant folding
and adding zero should make `goal` equivalent to `6` after running the rules.
The initial failing check confirms that declaring the rules alone is insufficient.

```elixir
math_theory = """
(datatype Expr (Num i64) (Var String) (Add Expr Expr) (Mul Expr Expr))
(rewrite (Add x (Num 0)) x)
(rewrite (Mul (Num a) (Num b))
         (Num (* a b)))
"""

example_input = """
(let goal (Add (Mul (Num 2) (Num 3)) (Num 0)))
(fail (check (= goal (Num 6))))
"""

simplify = """
(run 3)
(check (= goal (Num 6)))
"""

Kino.nothing()
```

## EGraph: change the graph, then roll back

Insert the expression, save a checkpoint, and run the rules. The simplified graph
contains both the original expression and its equivalent `6`; extraction selects
a representative without deleting alternatives. Pop restores the unsimplified
checkpoint, including its earlier equality information.

```elixir
Egglog.EGraph.with_egraph(math_theory, fn graph ->
  base = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)
  Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, example_input)
  inserted = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)
  :ok = Egglog.EGraph.push(graph)

  {simplified, representative} =
    try do
      Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, simplify)
      snapshot = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)
      {snapshot, Egglog.EGraph.extract!(graph, "goal")}
    after
      :ok = Egglog.EGraph.pop(graph)
    end

  Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, "(fail (check (= goal (Num 6))))")
  restored = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)

  Kino.Layout.grid([
    view.("Base: declarations and rules", base),
    view.("Expression inserted; rules not run", inserted),
    view.("Rules run inside the checkpoint", simplified),
    Kino.Markdown.new("Extracted representative: `" <> representative <> "`"),
    view.("After pop: back to the inserted expression", restored)
  ])
end)
```

The calls share state inside the callback. Keeping push/pop together, with pop in
`after`, makes rollback explicit even on failure. `with_egraph` closes the session
when the callback ends; rerunning this cell creates a fresh session.

## Program: repeat the experiment on independent clones

Load the same theory. One run inserts the expression; another inserts it and runs
the rules. A third inserts it again and confirms it is unsimplified: the preceding
run's equivalences did not carry over. A final empty run shows the unchanged base.
No push/pop is needed because every run starts from the loaded base.

```elixir
Egglog.Program.with_program(math_theory, fn program ->
  inserted = Egglog.Program.snapshot!(program, example_input, snapshot_options)

  simplified =
    Egglog.Program.snapshot!(
      program,
      example_input <> simplify <> "(extract goal)",
      snapshot_options
    )

  repeated = Egglog.Program.snapshot!(program, example_input, snapshot_options)
  base = Egglog.Program.snapshot!(program, "", snapshot_options)

  Kino.Layout.grid([
    view.("Run 1: expression inserted; rules not run", inserted),
    view.("Run 2: expression inserted and simplified", simplified),
    view.("Run 3: inserted again; no inherited simplification", repeated),
    view.("Base: still only declarations and rules", base)
  ])
end)
```

`with_program` closes the handle when the callback ends, including on failure.
Changing the Elixir theory string does not modify a loaded Program; load it again
to use the new theory. Return results, rather than handles, from either scoped API.

## Typed query results

Use `query!` for structured bindings rather than parsing extraction or print
output. Each binding records the native sort, value type and decoded value;
row order is unspecified. Both models below ask for the integer represented by
the simplified expression. The result is `6`.

A Program query supplies its own input: it does not read a preceding run's clone.
An EGraph query reads the session's current state. Matching itself does not run
persistent rules; run the required schedule first.

```elixir
source = example_input <> simplify

program_result =
  Egglog.Program.with_program(math_theory, fn program ->
    bindings = Egglog.Program.query!(program, source, [n: "i64"], ["(= goal (Num n))"])
    snapshot = Egglog.Program.snapshot!(program, source, snapshot_options)

    Kino.Layout.grid([
      Kino.Inspect.new(bindings),
      view.("Program query input on its own clone", snapshot)
    ])
  end)

egraph_result =
  Egglog.EGraph.with_egraph(math_theory, fn graph ->
    Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, source)
    bindings = Egglog.EGraph.query!(graph, [n: "i64"], ["(= goal (Num n))"])
    snapshot = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)

    Kino.Layout.grid([
      Kino.Inspect.new(bindings),
      view.("EGraph query reads current state", snapshot)
    ])
  end)

Kino.Layout.grid([program_result, egraph_result])
```

The Program query and its illustrative snapshot execute independently. In an
application, request the query result alone if you do not need a graphic.

## Handle errors and restore mutable state

Non-bang functions return `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, reason}`; bang functions
raise. A failed EGraph command sequence can leave earlier mutations in place.
Here rules run successfully before an intentionally wrong check fails. The error
does not undo simplification; popping the checkpoint does.

```elixir
Egglog.EGraph.with_egraph(math_theory, fn graph ->
  Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, example_input)
  before = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)
  :ok = Egglog.EGraph.push(graph)

  {message, after_error} =
    try do
      {:error, {:check_failed, message}} =
        Egglog.EGraph.run(graph, "(run 3)\n(check (= goal (Num 7)))")

      Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, "(check (= goal (Num 6)))")
      {message, Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)}
    after
      :ok = Egglog.EGraph.pop(graph)
    end

  Egglog.EGraph.run!(graph, "(fail (check (= goal (Num 6))))")
  restored = Egglog.EGraph.snapshot!(graph, snapshot_options)

  Kino.Layout.grid([
    view.("Before experiment", before),
    Kino.Text.new(message),
    view.("After failed check: simplification remains", after_error),
    view.("After pop: original state restored", restored)
  ])
end)
```

A failed Program run does not change the base: its work is clone-local. Failed
checks mean an equality was not established in that run, not that it is disproven.

Parser errors additionally expose a structured source location. Byte offsets are
zero-based UTF-8 positions; line and column coordinates are one-based. Source that
fails to parse has no graph to display.

```elixir
{:error, {:invalid_theory, message, location}} = Egglog.parse(")")
Kino.Layout.grid([Kino.Text.new(message), Kino.Inspect.new(location)])
```

## Resource ownership and application integration

Use `with_program` and `with_egraph` for bounded operations: both close in `after`,
even when the callback raises. Return values or snapshots, not handles that would
already be closed. For a long-lived service, use explicit `Program.load` or
`EGraph.new` and arrange matching `close` calls in the owning component's lifecycle.
An EGraph session also closes when its creator process exits.

Calls sharing a handle are serialized. Independent handles can execute concurrently;
`num_threads` configures native parallelism at creation/load time. A caller timeout
does not cancel native computation. Scope theories, inputs and schedules according
to the application's resource requirements.

A loaded Program does not follow changes to its Elixir source string. Load a new
base when changing a theory. Parsed `Egglog.Commands` can be reused to avoid repeated
parsing; they do not make Program runs share mutable state.

## Rendering, exports and limits

All graphs use `render: :auto`: native **DOT → Graphviz → SVG** when `dot` is
available, otherwise native **JSON → wrapper converter → Mermaid**. Kino supplies
the notebook display; the library itself has no Kino dependency.

Snapshot limits cap the serialized picture, not the reasoning work. Partial-view
notices report omitted data. Extraction chooses a representative without deleting
equivalent alternatives. Neither a bounded run count nor the output limit is a
general native time or memory limit.

Snapshots retain format-specific source: `dot`/`svg` for SVG snapshots and
`json`/`mermaid` for Mermaid snapshots. Save those strings directly, or use the
corresponding artifact-path options. `Egglog.Snapshot.to_mermaid/1` converts retained
JSON without rerunning egglog. Explicit `render: :dot` and `render: :json` need no
layout engine; SVG requires Graphviz, and Mermaid rendering needs a frontend.

The tutorial notebooks also use `Egglog.Program.Step` and `next.(previous, code)`
as replayable Livebook conveniences over Program.

See the [shared guide](https://github.com/davfuenmayor/egglog_elixir/blob/main/livebooks/README.md)
for option details and native-extension boundaries, or return to the [index](index.livemd)
for the language tutorial and other notebooks.
